WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.429
Do you know what's actually running inside your Java app?

2
00:00:04.981 --> 00:00:06.800
Meet BootUI.

3
00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:10.970
Your local developer console for Spring Boot and Quarkus.

4
00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:16.149
Add the starter or extension, and open the console in development.

5
00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:22.351
Start with Scorecard: a practical overview of your application, backed by advisor assessments.

6
00:00:22.491 --> 00:00:26.805
This gives you directly actionable best practices for your application.

7
00:00:26.945 --> 00:00:30.822
Open a finding to see the evidence and the suggested next step.

8
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:37.983
Now leave the static assessment and watch the running application.

9
00:00:38.123 --> 00:00:42.723
Live Activity brings captured events into one bounded, local journal.

10
00:00:42.863 --> 00:00:44.742
Select the order-preview request.

11
00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:49.893
This application returns success despite a downstream failure.

12
00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:57.860
Its profile connects the events that belong to this request: remote call, transaction, SQL, and error evidence.

13
00:00:58.983 --> 00:01:00.388
Start with REST Client.

14
00:01:00.528 --> 00:01:05.521
The actual outbound call reaches a local service and receives a server error.

15
00:01:05.661 --> 00:01:09.514
Its timing and request ownership connect it to our order preview.

16
00:01:10.617 --> 00:01:15.621
The Spring Transactions panel shows the enclosing transaction and its rollback.

17
00:01:15.761 --> 00:01:18.424
The remote call happened inside that boundary.

18
00:01:18.564 --> 00:01:22.207
That's an important clue, not a separate, unrelated event.

19
00:01:23.317 --> 00:01:28.601
SQL Trace shows the recorded query, its duration, and the code that issued it.

20
00:01:28.741 --> 00:01:35.023
Follow the actual execution instead of guessing from repository code or a static warning.

21
00:01:36.117 --> 00:01:38.987
And the error evidence shows the failed remote response.

22
00:01:39.127 --> 00:01:41.934
The controller logged it, then still returned success.

23
00:01:42.074 --> 00:01:43.897
Now that contradiction is visible.

24
00:01:45.017 --> 00:01:48.792
Runtime Insights goes beyond isolated panels.

25
00:01:48.912 --> 00:01:53.911
It connects captured evidence and surfaces patterns worth investigating.

26
00:01:54.031 --> 00:01:57.181
Here, a transaction spans a remote call.

27
00:01:57.301 --> 00:02:01.151
An error also sits behind a successful response.

28
00:02:01.271 --> 00:02:09.071
Powered by the unique BootUI event model and dedicated algorithms for finding correlations between events.

29
00:02:09.191 --> 00:02:14.840
Move from the pattern to the affected request, then inspect the underlying evidence.

30
00:02:15.917 --> 00:02:20.317
BootUI gives you unique insights into your JVM.

31
00:02:21.127 --> 00:02:24.652
Explore live metrics with Micrometer support.

32
00:02:24.772 --> 00:02:29.222
Analyze a heap dump to understand what's occupying memory.

33
00:02:29.767 --> 00:02:33.692
Inspect a thread dump to see what your threads are doing.

34
00:02:36.067 --> 00:02:44.641
And get a JVM Tuning guide, with runtime measurements, explanations, and suggested options for your application.

35
00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:49.557
Need deeper visibility?

36
00:02:49.697 --> 00:02:51.715
Attach the optional Java agent.

37
00:02:51.855 --> 00:02:53.908
The usual console works without it.

38
00:02:54.048 --> 00:03:00.300
With instrumentation enabled, Code Paths shows application methods and how work flows through them.

39
00:03:01.717 --> 00:03:03.072
Select a slow method.

40
00:03:03.537 --> 00:03:07.822
Choose Probe this method, inspect the capture settings, and start the probe.

41
00:03:17.567 --> 00:03:23.685
Capture the next calls, then inspect duration, outcome, caller, thread, and request identity.

42
00:03:25.067 --> 00:03:31.425
This is automatic JVM instrumentation giving you detailed insights on your running code.

43
00:03:34.417 --> 00:03:42.187
Side Effects reveals work that ordinary client panels can miss: an SDK's raw network call, file activity, and

44
00:03:42.187 --> 00:03:44.682
explicitly enabled thread activity.

45
00:03:44.822 --> 00:03:47.535
See the calling code and the request attribution.

46
00:03:47.675 --> 00:03:52.251
Distinguish captured facts from a diagnosis, and choose the sensors you need.

47
00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:56.477
Your AI coding agent can use this evidence too.

48
00:03:56.617 --> 00:04:01.879
Connect the local MCP server, and install the skill for the investigation workflow.

49
00:04:02.019 --> 00:04:06.193
The command-line interface provides another route to the same diagnostics.

50
00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:14.500
Here's a separate, real Hibernate example.

51
00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:22.020
GitHub Copilot uses native MCP tools to investigate an N+1 query pattern.

52
00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:29.311
The fix selects the existing JOIN FETCH query, loading orders and customers together instead of issuing

53
00:04:29.311 --> 00:04:30.564
secondary selects.

54
00:04:30.684 --> 00:04:35.684
Your AI Agent will then use this context to fix your code's issues.

55
00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:42.348
Run the same endpoint again and compare history.

56
00:04:42.488 --> 00:04:47.489
The measured SQL work drops from seventeen statements to one per request.

57
00:04:47.629 --> 00:04:51.556
All forty-nine checked responses retain the same JSON.

58
00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:54.093
BootUI.

59
00:04:54.213 --> 00:04:57.563
Runtime evidence for AI coding agents.

60
00:04:57.683 --> 00:04:59.556
Fully integrated.

61
00:04:59.676 --> 00:05:06.151
Explore the project on GitHub, and visit our website for the documentation and setup guide.
